{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>Bring her out and let her be burned.</b> The reason is because the court of Shem had decreed concerning relations with the nations, as they said (Avodah Zarah 36b) — and it implies even where there is no prohibition of forbidden relations, neither those prohibited to Noachides nor those prohibited to Israel, even so they decreed upon her. And perhaps there was a tradition in their hands to enact decrees and to put to death one who transgresses the decree. Therefore, when he [Judah] knew that the matter had been with himself, she was released, because the decree had been specifically upon relations with a gentile. And there was also no prohibition of forbidden relations in her case, for one's daughter-in-law is permitted to a Noachide. And according to their words when they said (Sotah 10b): ״וְלֹא יָסַף לְדַעְתָּהּ״ — that he did not cease from her again, this tells that the father [Judah] would perform levirate marriage with his daughter-in-law."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 38, :notes {0 "The phrase 'מִשּׁוּם דְּבֵית דִין שֶׁל שֵׁם גָּזְרוּ' is parsed as a causal clause: the reason for the burning is the decree of Shem's court. The Talmudic citations are given in their standard form with tractate and page."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:46:58.245690575Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}